{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · the Tangent Bridge","loop":30,"ease":"inout","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.4319784,0.0024291,-0.0471816,0.34084215]},{"t":0.62,"box":[-0.4319784,0.0024291,-0.0471816,0.34084215]},{"t":0.68,"box":[-0.258843361,0.00156039076,-0.000727901745,0.334105862]},{"t":0.76,"box":[-0.258843361,0.00156039076,-0.000727901745,0.334105862]},{"t":0.8,"box":[-0.4319784,0.0024291,-0.0471816,0.34084215]}],"stops":[0.14,0.3,0.48,0.62,0.76],"beats":[{"title":"A curve is a function on the circle","text":"Seen from its middle, a closed shape is one distance for every direction: a function *F* on the circle. This one has a little detail in it, waves of three, five and nine to a turn.\n\nThe theorem asks two questions about it. What do you have to keep of *F*, and what do you get back?"},{"title":"Read it at the atoms","text":"Read *F* in eight directions, evenly spaced, at the odd multiples of π/8. **Eight numbers are kept, and nothing else.**\n\nEach reading is taken on its own, in a fixed amount of work. None of them needs to know how many others there are, or what lies between. In the paper these directions are the *atoms*; in a .tvf file they are the leaves. [The forward map, §3](../papers.html#tb_classical:3.1-definition)"},{"title":"One kernel, turned to every atom","text":"To give the readings back as a curve, each one carries the same shape: the kernel K₈(φ) = sin 4φ ⁄ (8 tan ½φ).\n\nIt is **1 at its own atom and 0 at every other**. Here the bright petal touches the base circle exactly where the other seven rays cross it. The faint ones are the same kernel, turned to the other atoms. [The kernel, §4.2](../papers.html#tb_classical:4.2-kernel-properties)"},{"title":"Their sum passes through every atom","text":"Weight each kernel by its reading and add them up. The result is the violet curve.\n\nAt an atom only that atom's kernel is nonzero, so the sum there **is the reading, exactly**. Read the curve back in the eight directions and you get the eight numbers you started with, to the last digit. These are (R1) and (R2) of the theorem. [The statement, §5.1](../papers.html#tb_classical:5.1-statement)"},{"title":"Between the atoms: close, and bounded","text":"Between two atoms the curve is the kernels' best guess. Eight readings cannot hold waves of five and nine to a turn, so those come back as slower waves, and here, at the widest gap, the curve misses *F* by about 28 on a radius near 100.\n\nThe miss is never unbounded. It is at most a constant, times log *N*, times how far *F* itself moves within one atom's spacing (R3). A smooth *F* is missed by little, a rough one by more. [Bounded between, §6.3](../papers.html#tb_classical:6.3-r3-bounded-between)"},{"title":"Double the atoms, and the curve closes in","text":"With sixteen atoms, each of the eight slides half a step and eight more come in between them; with thirty-two, sixteen more. The lattice doubles, and the doubling map θ ↦ 2θ carries each lattice onto the one before.\n\nThe violet curve closes in on *F*. Thirty-two atoms hold every wave of fewer than sixteen to a turn, and the finest wave of *F* is nine, so at thirty-two **the curve is *F* exactly**. For any *F* smooth enough the curve converges as the atoms double (R4). At no step did the reading or the sum use anything but the readings themselves (R5). [Convergence, §6.4](../papers.html#tb_classical:6.4-r4-convergence-and-the-necessity-of-the-class)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.675,0.675,-0.675,-0.675],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":45},{"morph":0},{"morph":2},{"morph":3},{"morph":4},{"morph":5},{"morph":6},{"morph":7},{"morph":8},{"morph":9},{"morph":10},{"morph":11},{"morph":12},{"morph":13},{"morph":14},{"morph":15},{"morph":16},{"morph":17},{"morph":46},{"morph":48},{"morph":49},{"morph":50},{"morph":51},{"morph":